WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:03.136
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-nominated actor

00:00:03.136 --> 00:00:05.138
you know from shows
like "Shameless" and "The Bear."

00:00:05.138 --> 00:00:06.639
He stars in "The Iron Claw,"

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:08.575
which is in theaters
December 22nd.

00:00:08.575 --> 00:00:10.009
Let's take a look.

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:13.045
[ Indistinct shouting ]

00:00:13.045 --> 00:00:16.082
[ "Tom Sawyer" plays ]

00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:23.589
♪♪

00:00:23.589 --> 00:00:25.958
-[ Grunting ]

00:00:25.958 --> 00:00:30.130
♪♪

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:33.233
-♪ What you say
about his company ♪

00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:35.068
♪ Is what you say about... ♪

00:00:35.068 --> 00:00:38.304
-Please welcome back to the show
Jeremy Allen White!

00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:40.773
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:40.773 --> 00:00:43.776
[ Up-tempo music plays ]

00:00:43.776 --> 00:00:54.887
♪♪

00:00:54.887 --> 00:00:56.422
So happy you're back.

00:00:56.422 --> 00:00:58.157
-I'm happy to be back.
Thank you, man.

00:00:58.157 --> 00:01:00.360
-You know, I'm a huge fan
of the "Bear" soundtrack,

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.629
but this movie has some
real early '80s bangers in it.

00:01:03.629 --> 00:01:06.733
-Some good needle drop.
-Some real good needle drops.

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:08.902
It was a -- I mean, for me,
this was great

00:01:08.902 --> 00:01:11.737
because it was an era where
that music means a lot to me.

00:01:11.737 --> 00:01:13.539
And that was when,
you know, as a kid,

00:01:13.539 --> 00:01:16.008
I was into this wrestling world.
-Were you?

00:01:16.008 --> 00:01:17.577
-I was, but I think
maybe a little

00:01:17.577 --> 00:01:19.412
after the story
of these Von Erichs,

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:21.714
which is this is
sort of late '70s, early '80s.

00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:23.316
This is a real-life
wrestling family.

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:24.717
-Yes.
-Did you know anything

00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:26.218
about them before?
-I knew nothing,

00:01:26.218 --> 00:01:28.053
I had such, like,
peripheral knowledge

00:01:28.053 --> 00:01:30.790
of professional wrestling.
I had some friends into it

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:32.791
when I was young.
I never got into it.

00:01:32.791 --> 00:01:34.693
So my introduction
to professional wrestling

00:01:34.693 --> 00:01:36.896
was through the Von Erichs,
yeah.

00:01:36.896 --> 00:01:38.498
-And then
your other introduction is,

00:01:38.498 --> 00:01:41.167
you had to become
a Kerry Von Erich.

00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:43.636
So your first introduction
to this person is,

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:47.039
they showed you a picture.
-Sean, the director said,

00:01:47.039 --> 00:01:49.875
"Hey, I want you to do this
movie about these brothers,

00:01:49.875 --> 00:01:51.410
these wrestlers, really --"
-I was like,

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:53.412
"Amazing. I love this.
Let's talk about it."

00:01:53.412 --> 00:01:54.980
He was like, "Okay,
I want you to play Kerry."

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:57.950
So I was like, "Alright,
I'll Google 'Kerry Von Erich.'"

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:00.153
And I did.
-And then this came up.

00:02:00.153 --> 00:02:02.655
[ Laughter ]

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:04.924
-It's a large man.
-Yeah.

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:07.760
-I mean, people can
get in shape for roles, but --

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:10.530
-I can get in shape for a role.
-But people can't get taller.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.965
-No. I can't, uh...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:12.965 --> 00:02:15.834
I told Sean
I can try to act that big."

00:02:15.834 --> 00:02:17.203
-Yeah.
-"But I don't know

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:19.972
if I can act,
you know, two feet taller,

00:02:19.972 --> 00:02:22.008
200 pounds more."
You know, it would be...

00:02:22.008 --> 00:02:24.110
-You have your own words.
I read an interview.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:25.911
You described yourself
as a short king.

00:02:25.911 --> 00:02:28.114
-No, no, no, no, no, no.
Not my own words.

00:02:28.114 --> 00:02:29.715
-Not your own words?
Whose words?

00:02:29.715 --> 00:02:32.352
-This is very embarrassing
for me.

00:02:32.352 --> 00:02:34.888
I would not call myself that.
-Okay.

00:02:34.888 --> 00:02:38.324
-Cam, the journalist said --
or I was talking about Kerry,

00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:40.159
and I was like,
"He's a large man.

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:41.427
I'm not a large man."

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:42.896
And Cam, the journalist,
who I love,

00:02:42.896 --> 00:02:44.897
was like, "No,
you're a short king."

00:02:44.897 --> 00:02:47.734
-Gotcha.
-And I went, "S-Sure."

00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:49.235
-Yeah.
-And that was it.

00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:50.737
-But before I went
into your dressing room,

00:02:50.737 --> 00:02:53.005
everybody said, "Remember,
call him Short King."

00:02:53.005 --> 00:02:55.041
-Right. Which you did.
Thank you.

00:02:55.041 --> 00:02:56.576
-So, you might not
have said it first.

00:02:56.576 --> 00:02:57.810
-I don't want to say it,
but I do want everybody else

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.645
to say it.
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:59.645 --> 00:03:02.014
-You did, obviously --
And I mean,

00:03:02.014 --> 00:03:03.783
I think anybody
who's seen clips of it,

00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:06.452
obviously, Zac Efron
has transformed his body.

00:03:06.452 --> 00:03:09.355
You guys did put the time in.
And then you have to,

00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:12.625
obviously, become
believable wrestlers.

00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:13.893
-Correct.

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:15.861
-How much time
was spent training?

00:03:15.861 --> 00:03:19.032
-Not not enough -- for me,
at least, I think.

00:03:19.032 --> 00:03:22.501
Um, but let's see,
we had about two months

00:03:22.501 --> 00:03:25.871
to exercise and eat a lot
and try to get bigger.

00:03:25.871 --> 00:03:29.108
And then we shot the film
in Baton Rouge in Louisiana,

00:03:29.108 --> 00:03:31.410
and we got there maybe
three weeks early

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:33.946
and worked with this
really amazing wrestler,

00:03:33.946 --> 00:03:35.348
Chavo Guerrero.

00:03:35.348 --> 00:03:38.517
Um, and he took us
through the steps.

00:03:38.517 --> 00:03:39.852
It was kind of like
a crash course

00:03:39.852 --> 00:03:43.623
in wrestling.
And like learning any skill,

00:03:43.623 --> 00:03:45.091
at a certain point,
you kind of just have to,

00:03:45.091 --> 00:03:47.393
like, jump and hope and pray.
-I feel like

00:03:47.393 --> 00:03:49.696
you've just been through this
because, obviously,

00:03:49.696 --> 00:03:51.497
in "The Bear,"
you're playing a chef.

00:03:51.497 --> 00:03:53.733
There's a lot of chefs who are
going to have opinions about it

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:56.002
and are going to
appreciate authenticity

00:03:56.002 --> 00:03:57.570
and call you out if it's not.
-Sure.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.472
-I would imagine
wrestling is the same,

00:03:59.472 --> 00:04:02.442
if not more so.
Have you received feedback

00:04:02.442 --> 00:04:04.676
yet from people
in the world of wrestling?

00:04:04.676 --> 00:04:07.713
-I haven't -- I haven't heard
a lot of opinions yet.

00:04:07.713 --> 00:04:10.917
And so I'm assuming
that's a good thing

00:04:10.917 --> 00:04:12.284
if nobody's, like,
yelling at me.

00:04:12.284 --> 00:04:14.453
-In the modern era,
if people have

00:04:14.453 --> 00:04:16.055
negative opinions,
they will find you.

00:04:16.055 --> 00:04:17.757
-They'll find you
and let it be known.

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:20.560
Um, no. But, yeah,
you're absolutely right.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.896
I mean, I think the fans
for professional wrestling,

00:04:23.896 --> 00:04:25.731
the story of the Von Erichs,
these guys,

00:04:25.731 --> 00:04:27.833
like, I wasn't
familiar with them.

00:04:27.833 --> 00:04:30.403
But I think, like,
all of the big guys today

00:04:30.403 --> 00:04:32.838
and in the past decades
were just, like,

00:04:32.838 --> 00:04:35.107
massive fans of the Von Erichs.

00:04:35.107 --> 00:04:36.575
And I think
they really inspired,

00:04:36.575 --> 00:04:37.910
like, all the guys
doing it today.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:41.781
-It's also, you know --
it is a really moving film.

00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:44.182
Uh, it's a wonderful film
about family,

00:04:44.182 --> 00:04:45.551
but it's a true life.

00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:48.054
The story is incredibly tragic
about these brothers.

00:04:48.054 --> 00:04:52.258
And, you know,
Kevin, who Zac plays.

00:04:52.258 --> 00:04:54.426
-Yeah.
-I know he was involved.

00:04:54.426 --> 00:04:55.894
And obviously
there are other Von Erichs

00:04:55.894 --> 00:04:57.396
who are still with us.

00:04:57.396 --> 00:04:59.865
Was sharing the film with them
and having their hands in it --

00:04:59.865 --> 00:05:01.266
that must have been
a very important piece of it.

00:05:01.266 --> 00:05:02.834
-That was major, yeah.

00:05:02.834 --> 00:05:05.371
Kevin Von Erich
is still with us,

00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:07.439
and he wasn't
incredibly involved

00:05:07.439 --> 00:05:09.008
in the process of filming,

00:05:09.008 --> 00:05:11.711
but he did give Sean Durkin,
our director, his blessing.

00:05:11.711 --> 00:05:15.047
Um, and all he asked
was to make sure

00:05:15.047 --> 00:05:18.217
that it was clear
how much he loved his brothers

00:05:18.217 --> 00:05:20.786
and how much they loved
one another.

00:05:20.786 --> 00:05:24.890
Um, "As long as you do that,
I'll enjoy the film," you know?

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:26.826
And I think Sean
delivered on that.

00:05:26.826 --> 00:05:30.263
And it was something that Zac
and Harris and Stanley and I,

00:05:30.263 --> 00:05:33.565
who play all the brothers
were aware of

00:05:33.565 --> 00:05:34.967
through the process
of making it.

00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:37.002
Like, "If we can just, like,
forge this bond

00:05:37.002 --> 00:05:38.938
and make it feel
true and honest,

00:05:38.938 --> 00:05:40.439
um,
then we've done a good job."

00:05:40.439 --> 00:05:41.908
-I thought you really did.

00:05:41.908 --> 00:05:45.345
I thought that part of it,
the sibling-affection

00:05:45.345 --> 00:05:46.946
part of it came through clear.
-Yeah.

00:05:46.946 --> 00:05:49.181
-You, again, didn't strike me
as a lot of CGI in this film,

00:05:49.181 --> 00:05:50.683
that when you were
jumping off the top rope,

00:05:50.683 --> 00:05:51.917
that's you jumping off
the top rope.

00:05:51.917 --> 00:05:53.151
-That's all of us.
-I imagine,

00:05:53.151 --> 00:05:54.854
like, you think, "I could
jump off the top rope,"

00:05:54.854 --> 00:05:56.856
then you get up there
and I imagine

00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:58.791
it looks a little bit higher
than you thought.

00:05:58.791 --> 00:06:01.526
-Yeah. It's frightening.
It's really frightening.

00:06:01.526 --> 00:06:04.230
But I guess, yeah, like I said,
it's like anything.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:05.664
It was the same thing
with cooking for me.

00:06:05.664 --> 00:06:07.633
You know, I didn't feel like
I was particularly good at that.

00:06:07.633 --> 00:06:09.502
But at a certain point,
you kind of just have to,

00:06:09.502 --> 00:06:12.438
um -- you have to jump and hope.
And we had we had

00:06:12.438 --> 00:06:14.840
really amazing guys,
like, real wrestlers

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:16.475
that we were training with
and shooting with,

00:06:16.475 --> 00:06:19.011
like the guys in that clip
who played the Freebirds.

00:06:19.011 --> 00:06:21.714
Um, and they protected us.
You know?

00:06:21.714 --> 00:06:23.616
They made us look good.
-It's true.

00:06:23.616 --> 00:06:25.517
Like, ultimately, you have to,
in both "The Bear" and this,

00:06:25.517 --> 00:06:26.986
I mean,
you have to be confident.

00:06:26.986 --> 00:06:28.888
Like, if you're on the top rope,
you can't be like, [Whimpering]

00:06:28.888 --> 00:06:30.122
-Exactly, exactly.

00:06:30.122 --> 00:06:31.456
And if you're holding
a very sharp knife...

00:06:31.456 --> 00:06:33.191
-Yeah, cutting a carrot.
"The Bear" would be so bad

00:06:33.191 --> 00:06:34.593
if every time you cut a carrot,
you're like, "Aah!"

00:06:34.593 --> 00:06:36.162
-Yeah, exactly, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:36.162 --> 00:06:37.629
-I have a bunch of questions
about "The Bear."

00:06:37.629 --> 00:06:39.899
We're going to be right back
with Jeremy after this.

